[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should notifications reach users? → In-app only, durable records (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246m· How fresh does the in-app bell need to be? → Polling (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the semantic core: what "care about" and "change" actually mean. These two decide whether the feature feels useful
  or becomes noise people mute.

  [1mOn "care about":[0m the failure mode at each end is different. Pure explicit watching (a star button) means an empty
  inbox by default — nobody gets the notification they most needed because nobody thought to subscribe in advance. Pure
  implicit (you're auto-subscribed by being creator/assignee/commenter) means correct-by-default but inescapable: the
  one noisy task you can't get out of. I'd recommend [1mimplicit with a manual override[0m — auto-subscribe on involvement,
  plus explicit watch/unwatch so people can opt into tasks they're not on and out of ones they are. It's the GitHub
  model, and it's the model because the two pure forms each fail badly.

  [1mOn "change":[0m notifying on every field write is the fastest way to get the bell ignored — a typo fix in a description
  shouldn't ping four people. I'd recommend a [1mnamed set of significant events[0m (status change, assignee change, deletion,
  comment) rather than diffing all fields. Naming them also makes the notification text writable: "Alex assigned you
  [3mFix login[0m" needs to know [3mwhich[0m kind of change happened, which a generic field-diff can't tell you.

  One rule I'd build in regardless: [1mnever notify you of your own action.[0m It's the single most common complaint about
  notification systems and it's much easier to enforce from the start than to retrofit.
